  about notification details, I need to know what we're actually building on — otherwise I'd be designing delivery for
  events that nothing emits.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is a single-user browser app or a real multi-user service. It's not cosmetic — it decides
  whether "notify" means an in-page toast against local state, or a server that owns task changes, fans out to
  subscribers, and persists an unread state per user. Those share almost no code.

  - [1mSingle-user, client-side[0m — cheap, works today, no backend or accounts. Buys a usable app fast. Costs: "users" is a
    fiction, so anything cross-user (assignment, mentions, email) is off the table, and adding a server later means
    rewriting the event and storage layer.
  - [1mMulti-user service[0m — matches the words in your request literally. Buys real per-user subscriptions and delivery you
    can extend to email/push. Costs: you need a backend, a datastore, and auth before a single notification fires.
    That's three projects ahead of this one.
  - [1mMulti-user, but staged[0m — design the task/event model now so it's server-shaped, build the first slice client-side
    against a stubbed API. Costs some upfront design discipline; keeps the rewrite small.

  My recommendation is the third, [3mif[0m multi-user is genuinely the goal — but I'd rather hear that it isn't and build the
  simple thing honestly.
